-\section{Related work}
+Repository-level benchmarks evaluate patches against existing applications. SWE-bench uses real issue descriptions~\cite{jimenez2024swebench}, and SWE-Bench Pro includes longer engineering tasks spanning multiple files~\cite{deng2025swepro}. These settings motivate evaluating a feature change through its behavior in the application. A controlled comparison can then ask whether changing the repository supplied to an agent affects its ability to satisfy the same requirements.
+
+SmartKit is a Next.js starter kit with authentication, billing, and email components, developed in the preceding thesis~\cite{trieu2026thesis}. That evaluation examined coding-assistant adherence to conventions, including file placement, type and schema usage, and required components. The present study asks whether concrete starter-kit design choices affect an agent's ability to complete a feature change while preserving applicable behavior.
+
+Prior work gives reasons to examine repository organization and verification separately. Repository instructions have mixed effects on task success~\cite{gloaguen2026agents}; existing code and its placement can affect implementation decisions~\cite{zhu2026precedent}; and structural requirements can affect performance alongside executable behavior~\cite{dente2026constraint}. Bundling layout, instructions, examples, and tests would make an observed difference difficult to attribute to a particular intervention.
+
+We compare feature-based and layer-based source organization, each with and without an additional package of runnable checks for existing behavior. The primary endpoint is joint success: a patch passes both acceptance and preservation checks in a final evaluator withheld from the agent. Task requirements and ordinary tool permissions are shared across conditions, including permission to write and run tests. The verification intervention changes access to the supplied package.
+
+FeatureArc and CodeThread already evaluate new and retained behavior, and controlled verification-surface studies examine agents' available tools~\cite{wang2026featurearc,patel2026codethread,mehta2026verification}. Our question concerns a matched organization-by-package comparison within the same initial application. Its value as an extension or purposeful replication still requires comparison with the closest versioned artifacts. The factorial design and joint endpoint alone establish no novelty claim.
+
+The intended contribution is an estimate of these two concrete intervention effects within a qualified SmartKit task set, with uncertainty and execution costs. This draft reports completed development qualification and a four-session smoke pilot, then specifies the remaining main-study design. Main-study outcomes are not yet available. Conclusions about other repositories or human maintenance require separate evidence.
