+\section{Results}\label{sec:results}
+The available results concern development qualification and one smoke-pilot task. Main-study collection has not begun. Check counts describe evaluator cases; the four model sessions describe attempts on one task.
+
+\subsection{Baseline and evaluator qualification}\label{sec:qualification-results}
+Both repository organizations passed TypeScript checking, production compilation, and the same 14 existing unit/component tests. In the initial layout qualification, each unsolved snapshot passed ten baseline integration checks using local HTTP requests and PostgreSQL. Applying the corresponding history-filter reference yielded 15 passing integration checks in each layout. After removing the reference, the ten baseline checks passed again. This established comparability within those exercised contracts.
+
+The initial development evaluator rejected three deliberately faulty filter implementations and accepted a correct alternative. History and status checks used real HTTP sessions. Direct server-action checks used real SQL with session lookup and cache invalidation stubbed. These observations establish sensitivity to the exercised faults; they do not qualify browser interaction or production database connectivity.
+
+The subsequent smoke-pilot evaluator was qualified separately before model execution. Both unsolved baselines passed preservation and failed acceptance. Both reference implementations passed both components. The V1 package passed all ten visible checks on each unsolved baseline. Its final 29-check evaluator remained unchanged from qualification through scoring. The initial ten/15-check layout checks and the later 29-check pilot evaluator are separate instruments.
+
+\subsection{Smoke-pilot outcomes}\label{sec:pilot-results}
+All four agent sessions completed within the allocated wall-time cap and produced patches that passed the final evaluator (Table~\ref{tab:pilot}). Neither an agent timeout nor an unsuccessful agent outcome occurred. The two V1 session records each contained one invocation of the supplied behavioral-check command. Invocation establishes use of the command; it does not establish that its feedback changed a patch.
