 \section{Discussion and threats to validity}
-The proposed design can estimate the effect of concrete starter-kit interventions within qualified tasks. It cannot establish that feature-based organization is universally preferable. Moving modules changes import paths and the evidence an agent encounters; those changes are part of the intervention. Neutral instructions and a transformation manifest reduce accidental confounding, but do not decompose locality from every other aspect of repository organization.
-
-The verification comparison also has a defined boundary. Its additional tests expose examples and consume execution resources, so a positive result would support the package under the stated budget. It would not identify feedback as the sole cause. An adverse result could arise from execution overhead, distracting examples, or ineffective repair. Trace analysis can help distinguish these explanations without converting them into proven mechanisms.
-
-The author developed SmartKit and will help construct the tasks and evaluator. This creates a risk of favoring familiar paths or requirements. Path-independent outcomes, alternative valid solutions, and independent review where feasible will address specific parts of that risk. Evaluator sensitivity remains limited to the tested contracts, even after these checks.
-
-One repository and a billing-heavy candidate pool limit external validity. Initial conclusions must name the evaluated task set, snapshot, model/harness, and budget. Replication on an independently selected repository would test whether an observed effect transfers beyond SmartKit. A separate human study would be needed to assess maintainability for developers. Neither transfer nor human productivity follows automatically from higher joint success in this experiment.
+\subsection{What the development evidence establishes}
+The completed workflow connects a public contract to isolated execution and post-session scoring in all four conditions. This supports further instrument development. Whether organization or supplied checks improve success across the intended workload remains open.
+
+The present evidence provides a local feasibility record for a comparison motivated by prior work on new and retained behavior, repository context, and verification access. Positioning the study as an extension or purposeful replication requires the closest artifact-level comparisons. Insufficient additional understanding would lead to a documented narrowing of the question or contribution before main collection.
+
+\subsection{Intervention interpretation}
+Relocation changes import paths and the evidence an agent encounters. Neutral instructions and a transformation manifest reduce accidental confounding, while leaving locality and traversal exposure combined. Any effect would apply to these two organizations under the recorded conditions.
+
+The verification intervention combines examples, feedback, and runtime overhead. An adverse result could reflect overhead, distracting examples, or ineffective repair. Traces may distinguish these interpretations. Isolating feedback would require an information-matched control.
+
+\subsection{Evaluator scope and author involvement}
+The author's involvement in SmartKit, task construction, and evaluation can favor familiar requirements or paths. Path-independent checks and valid/faulty alternatives address specific evaluator weaknesses. Independent review and second coding are currently unavailable, leaving shared-author bias unresolved. Assistant checks provide no independent scholarly review.
+
+HTTP/DOM checks and session/cache stubs leave browser/authentication behavior partly untested. New tasks need contract-appropriate checks. Session revocation specifically requires qualification of next-request invalidation before expanded-pilot admission.
+
+\subsection{Task coverage, uncertainty, and transfer}
+The billing-heavy inventory includes related history-workflow tasks, so equal task weighting could overweight one family. Domain/family records and sensitivity checks will support a bounded interpretation. Proposed profile and session tasks add development coverage only after qualification.
+
+Conclusions will name the evaluated tasks, snapshot, model/harness, and budget. Another independently selected repository would test transfer beyond SmartKit; developer maintenance requires a human study.
+
+Caching, infrastructure, and model drift may affect timing. With no repetitions within a cell, the smoke pilot cannot characterize that variation. Main precision planning must account for repetition and batch dependence. Insufficient feasible precision will lead to an exploratory scope declared before collection, with inconclusive estimates reported as such.
 
 \section{Conclusion}
-We propose a controlled study of repository organization and behavioral verification during agent-driven SaaS feature extension. SmartKit provides an existing application from which matched conditions can be constructed, while independent acceptance and preservation checks define the outcome. The next empirical step is to qualify the baseline and task evaluators. Conclusions about intervention effects will require fresh, fully archived trials under a frozen protocol.
-
-\clearpage
+Development qualification and a four-session smoke pilot established an executable workflow for comparing SmartKit source organization and supplied behavioral checks on one history-filter task. Every pilot patch passed the final evaluator within its stated coverage. Comparative effects on extension success, preservation, and cost remain unanswered. Task qualification and precision planning must precede a frozen main protocol and fresh trials. A traceable study archive is being prepared for reproducibility, with public release subject to redistribution rights and author review.
